# Bad instruments, weak instruments
subtitle: "Why distance to a heart hospital can serve as an instrument, how one leak or a weak first stage breaks it, and why F above 10 is not enough."
tags: ["instrumental variables","weak instruments","exclusion restriction","first-stage f","heart attack","health economics"]
teaches: ["methods/instrumental-variables","methods/weak-instruments","concepts/exclusion-restriction"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow"}
next: {"title":"Selection on observables: matching, regression, and the leap of faith","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/selection-on-observables-matching-regression-and-the-leap-of-faith.cast"}

## Distance as a lottery
voice: male
level: advanced
sources:
    - id: mcclellan1994
      title: Does more intensive treatment of acute myocardial infarction in the elderly reduce mortality? Analysis using instrumental variables
      authors: McClellan, McNeil and Newhouse, JAMA
      year: 1994
      doi: 10.1001/jama.1994.03520110039026
      finding: 'Medicare AMI 1987–91: at most 5 points, gained on day one'

    icon house size 110 x 110 y 640 or ["home"] icon_look picture of house hidden true
    icon hosp size 110 x 470 y 640 or ["building"] icon_look picture of hospital hidden true
    arrow dist #4f8a4b from.x 185 from.y 640 to.x 395 to.y 640 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    text dist_lab "miles" #4f8a4b x 290 y 690 font_size 38 hidden true
    text hosp_lab "cath lab" #2f6b8f x 640 y 640 font_size 38 hidden true
    text nv_head "Died" x 820 y 500 font_size 44 hidden true
    text nv_r1 "Catheterized" #2f6b8f x 330 y 420 font_size 46 hidden true
    text nv_r1v "150" #2f6b8f x 820 y 420 font_size 56 hidden true
    text nv_r0 "Not" #b5482e x 330 y 330 font_size 46 hidden true
    text nv_r0v "350" #b5482e x 820 y 330 font_size 56 hidden true
    text nv_gap "−200" #8a5fa8 x 820 y 225 font_size 60 hidden true
    path nv_rule #8f887c points [[200,280],[960,280]] style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    text nv_per "per 1,000" #8f887c x 330 y 500 font_size 36 hidden true
    text h_cath "Catheterized" #2f6b8f x 590 y 520 font_size 42 hidden true
    text h_died "Died" x 900 y 520 font_size 42 hidden true
    path rule1 thick #3d3833 points [[60,485],[1065,485]] hidden true
    text r_near "Near" #4f8a4b x 190 y 430 font_size 50 hidden true
    text r_far "Far" #4f8a4b x 190 y 345 font_size 50 hidden true
    text near_c "260" #2f6b8f x 590 y 430 font_size 56 hidden true
    text far_c "200" #2f6b8f x 590 y 345 font_size 56 hidden true
    text near_d "300" x 900 y 430 font_size 56 hidden true
    text far_d "303" x 900 y 345 font_size 56 hidden true
    path rule2 #8f887c points [[60,298],[1065,298]] style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    text r_gap "Gap" x 190 y 250 font_size 46 hidden true
    text gap_c "+60" #2f6b8f x 590 y 250 font_size 60 hidden true
    text gap_d "−3" x 900 y 250 font_size 60 hidden true
    text fs_lab "first stage" #8f887c x 590 y 195 font_size 34 hidden true
    text rf_lab "reduced form" #8f887c x 900 y 195 font_size 34 hidden true
    math ratio tex "\\hat\\tau = \\frac{-3}{60} = -0.05" size 50 colors {"60":"#2f6b8f","\\hat\\tau":"#8a5fa8"} x 470 y 85 hidden true
    text five "−5 points" #8a5fa8 cites ["mcclellan1994"] x 930 y 85 font_size 54 hidden true
    figure fig aspect 3:2 members ["house","hosp","dist","dist_lab","hosp_lab","nv_head","nv_r1","nv_r1v","nv_r0","nv_r0v","nv_gap","nv_rule","nv_per","h_cath","h_died","rule1","r_near","r_far","near_c","far_c","near_d","far_d","rule2","r_gap","gap_c","gap_d","fs_lab","rf_lab","ratio","five"] hidden true

    card title "Distance as a lottery"

A: Live a few miles closer to a hos (@draw dist dist_lab@)pital with a cardiac catheteriza (@draw hosp hosp_lab@)tion lab, and after a heart attack you are more likely to get the procedure. Can that accident  (@highlight house effect circle@)of address tell us whether the procedure saves lives?
    draw house

B: Why bother with geography? Why not just compare the patients wh (@draw nv_r1@)o got the procedure with t (@draw nv_r0@)hose who didn't?
    draw nv_per nv_head

A: Here is what that comparison looks like, in numbers made up to be easy. Of a tho (@draw nv_r1v@)usand catheterized patients, a hundred and fifty die within a year; of a thousa (@draw nv_r0v@)nd who weren't, three hundred and fifty.

B: Two hundred fewer deaths per thousand, twenty points. On those numbers the procedure is a miracle.
    draw nv_rule nv_gap

A: Mostly it's a selection. Cardiologists send to the lab the patients strong enough to go, and thos (@highlight nv_gap effect circle@)e patients would have lived longer anyway.
    highlight nv_r1 nv_r1v

    erase nv_per nv_head nv_r1 nv_r0 nv_r1v nv_r0v nv_rule nv_gap hosp_lab dist_lab

A: So McClellan, McNeil and Newhouse sorted Medicare heart-attack patients aged sixty-five and over, from 1987 to 1991, by distance instead, and followed their survival for up to four years.
    draw rule1 r_near r_far

A: Near means the nearest catheterization hospital is hardly farther than the nearest hospital of any kind. Far means it is a real detour.
    highlight dist

B: And distance really changes who gets the procedure?
    draw h_cath

A: It does. Keeping to round numbers, two hundred and sixty of every thousand near patients are catheterized, against two hundred of the far ones.
    draw near_c far_c

A: That's sixty extra procedures per thousand, caused by nothing but the address: the first stage. We also assume living closer never makes anyone less likely to get it.
    draw rule2 r_gap gap_c fs_lab

B: If the procedure works, th (@highlight r_near@)e near group should die less. Do they?
    draw h_died

A: A little. Three hundred deaths against three hundred and three.
    draw near_d far_d

A: Three fewer deaths per thousand,  (@highlight gap_d effect circle@)which is called the reduced form.
    draw gap_d rf_lab

A: Now divide. Three fewer deaths, spread over sixty extra procedures, is one life for every twenty patients the distance moved.
    draw ratio

A: Five percentage points, for the patients whose treatment hung on their address. Our counts are made up; the real study found a survival gain of at most five points, up to four years on.
    draw five

A: That ratio is the instrumental-variables estimate: the jump in deaths between the groups, over the jump in treatment.
    morph ratio tex "\\hat\\tau = \\frac{E[Y \\mid Z=1] - E[Y \\mid Z=0]}{E[D \\mid Z=1] - E[D \\mid Z=0]}"

B: Five points, not twenty. But you're assuming those three  (@highlight gap_d@)deaths came only through the sixty procedures.
    highlight five effect circle

    fade near_c far_c near_d far_d to 0.35

A: Yes, and everything hangs on that word, only. A hospital with a lab is more than a lab.
    highlight hosp effect circle

## The arrow that must not exist
voice: male
level: advanced
sources:
    - id: mcclellan1994
      title: Does more intensive treatment of acute myocardial infarction in the elderly reduce mortality? Analysis using instrumental variables
      authors: McClellan, McNeil and Newhouse, JAMA
      year: 1994
      doi: 10.1001/jama.1994.03520110039026
      finding: Gain arose on day one, before the procedures

    box nz "Distance" #4f8a4b x 170 y 470 font_size 44 hidden true
    box nd "Cath lab" #2f6b8f x 560 y 470 font_size 44 hidden true
    box ny "Death" x 960 y 470 font_size 44 hidden true
    box nu "Frailty" dashed #8f887c x 760 y 670 font_size 40 hidden true
    arrow a_zd nz -> nd #4f8a4b style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    arrow a_dy nd -> ny style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    arrow a_ud nu -> nd dashed #8f887c hidden true
    arrow a_uy nu -> ny dashed #8f887c hidden true
    path l_zu dashed #8f887c points [[230,520],[670,670]] hidden true
    text q_zu "?" #8f887c x 430 y 625 font_size 56 hidden true
    text x_zu "✕" #4f8a4b cites ["mcclellan1994"] x 450 y 595 font_size 60 hidden true
    path a_zy smooth dashed thick #b5482e points [[170,425],[260,320],[560,285],[860,320],[935,410]] hidden true
    arrow a_zy_tip thick #b5482e from.x 915 from.y 380 to.x 945 to.y 425 hidden true
    text l_zy "day-one care" #b5482e cites ["mcclellan1994"] x 560 y 245 font_size 40 hidden true
    math leak tex "{\\hat\\tau} = 0 + \\frac{-3}{{60}} = -0.05" size 50 colors {"{60}":"#2f6b8f","{6}":"#2f6b8f","{\\delta}":"#b5482e","{\\pi}":"#2f6b8f","{\\hat\\tau}":"#8a5fa8","{\\tau}":"#8a5fa8"} x 560 y 95 hidden true
    figure fig aspect 3:2 members ["nz","nd","ny","nu","a_zd","a_dy","a_ud","a_uy","l_zu","q_zu","x_zu","a_zy","l_zy","leak","a_zy_tip"] hidden true

    card title "The arrow that must not exist"

A: Draw the study as arrows.  (@draw a_zd nd@)Distance pushes some patients into the l (@draw a_dy ny@)ab, and the lab may change who dies.
    draw nz

A: Frailty, which no claims form records, pushes on both: it ke (@highlight a_ud@)eps people out of the lab, and  (@highlight a_uy@)it kills them. That's the poison in the naive twenty points.
    draw nu a_ud a_uy

B: And distance gets round frailty how?
    highlight nu

A: Only if two things hold. First, distance must have nothing to do with frailty, as if each add (@highlight nz@)ress had been drawn by lottery.
    draw l_zu q_zu

A: The authors' case was that few people choose a home by its distance to a cath lab, and that near and (@draw x_zu@) far patients looked alike on the health measures in their records.
    erase q_zu

A: Second, distance may reach death only through the lab, with no other road from the address to survival. That's the exclusion restriction.
    draw a_zy a_zy_tip

B: That one sounds harder. A hospital with a lab is usually a big, busy hospital.
    highlight a_zy

A: It is, and it may do everything faster on the first day, not just catheterize. Far patients also t (@highlight nz effect circle@)end to be rural, a long way from every kind of care.
    draw l_zy

A: And the study found a trace of exactly this. The survival gain showed up on the first day in hospital, too early to be the procedures, and the authors put it down to other treatments.
    highlight l_zy effect circle

B: So how much damage can a small leak like that do?
    fade nu a_ud a_uy l_zu x_zu to 0.3

A: Back to our thousand patients. Suppose all three fewer deaths came from first-day care, and the lab itself saves no one.
    draw leak

A: The ratio can't tell the difference. It still divides three by sixty and reports five points, for a procedure that does nothing.
    highlight leak part "{60}"

A: In general, the estimate is the true effect plus the leak, delta, divided by the first stage, pi.
    morph leak tex "{\\hat\\tau} = {\\tau} + \\frac{{\\delta}}{{\\pi}}"

B: So the smaller the first stage, the bigger the same leak becomes.
    highlight leak part "{\\pi}"

A: Divide the same three deaths by six extra procedures instead of sixty, and the leak turns into fifty points.
    morph leak tex "{\\hat\\tau} = 0 + \\frac{{-3}}{{6}} = -0.5"

    quiz "A study uses each doctor's choice for the previous patient, drug A or B, as an instrument for the drug the next patient gets. Which story would break the exclusion restriction?" intro "A new case." voice b
        right: The follow-up: it is a second road from the doctor's habit to the patient's health that skips the drug, so the ratio would book it as the drug's effect.
        wrong: Two of these are reasons the instrument works. Which one opens a road to health that skips the drug?
        * Doctors' habits decide which drug a patient gets
        * Which patient came before has nothing to do with this patient's health
        + Doctors who favour drug A also follow patients up more closely

A: That's why exclusion is an argument, never a test result. The data show the first stage, but the road from address to death never shows up in them.
    highlight a_zy

## How strong is strong enough?
voice: male
level: advanced
sources:
    - id: staiger1997
      title: Instrumental Variables Regression with Weak Instruments
      authors: Staiger and Stock, Econometrica
      year: 1997
      finding: 'Rule of thumb: first-stage F below 10 is weak'
    - id: lee2022
      title: Valid t-ratio Inference for IV
      authors: Lee, McCrary, Moreira and Porter, American Economic Review
      year: 2022
      finding: True 5% t-test needs F > 104.7; SEs often much larger
    - id: angrist2024
      title: 'One instrument to rule them all: The bias and coverage of just-ID IV'
      authors: Angrist and Kolesár, Journal of Econometrics
      year: 2024
      finding: Just-ID bias usually small; F pretests distort
    - id: finkelstein2012
      title: 'The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year'
      authors: Finkelstein et al., Quarterly Journal of Economics
      year: 2012
      finding: Winning raised Medicaid coverage about 25 points

    math fs tex "\\hat\\pi = {60} \\pm {19}" size 52 colors {"{60}":"#2f6b8f","{19}":"#8f887c"} x 500 y 620 hidden true
    path st_axis thick #3d3833 points [[100,450],[900,450]] hidden true
    text st_t0 "0" #8f887c x 100 y 405 font_size 32 hidden true
    text st_t60 "60" #8f887c x 500 y 405 font_size 32 hidden true
    text st_t120 "120" #8f887c x 900 y 405 font_size 32 hidden true
    text st_cap "extra procedures per 1,000" #8f887c x 500 y 360 font_size 32 hidden true
    shape st_dot #2f6b8f shape circle x 500 y 450 width 30 height 30 style.fill #2f6b8f hidden true
    path st_bar #2f6b8f points [[246.7,450],[753.3,450]] style.stroke_width 8 hidden true
    text st_bar_lab "± 2 standard errors" #2f6b8f x 500 y 500 font_size 34 hidden true
    math tau_head tex "{\\hat\\tau}" size 48 colors {"{\\hat\\tau}":"#8a5fa8"} x 100 y 260 hidden true
    text tau_lo "−14" #8a5fa8 x 246.7 y 260 font_size 54 hidden true
    text tau_mid "−5" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 260 font_size 54 hidden true
    text tau_hi "−3" #8a5fa8 x 753.3 y 260 font_size 54 hidden true
    scale fscale at.percent [50,50] cites ["lee2022"] font_size 32 min 1 max 1000 value 104.7 log true markers [{"value":10,"label":"rule of thumb"},{"value":1000,"label":"Oregon ➜"}] hidden true
    text crit "at F = 10: 3.43, not 1.96" #8a5fa8 cites ["lee2022"] x 500 y 250 font_size 38 hidden true
    text camp1 "Raise the bar" #2f6b8f cites ["lee2022"] x 290 y 250 font_size 40 hidden true
    text camp1n "Lee et al. 2022" #8f887c x 290 y 205 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "How strong is strong enough?"

A: The ratio has a second weak spot, underneath it: the sixty extra proc (@draw st_dot@)edures are themselves an estimate, with noise.
    draw fs st_axis st_t0 st_t60 st_t120 st_cap

A: With all of Medicare behind it, the 1994 study had a strong first stage. But run the same design in a single county, two thou (@draw st_bar st_bar_lab@)sand patients, and sixty per thousand comes with a standard error of about nineteen.

B: Nineteen either way of sixty. So in another sample, the jump could come out anywhere from about twenty to a hundred.
    highlight st_bar

A: Now look at the ratio underneath. Three deaths over sixty procedu (@draw tau_hi@)res is five points; over a hundred, about three (@draw tau_lo@); but over twenty-two, fourteen. The ratio swi (@highlight tau_lo effect circle@)ngs hardest on the low side.
    draw tau_head tau_mid

A: Worse, the swings lean one way. In a small sample the same hidden frailty jiggles the first stage and the deaths together, so the noise drags the estimate toward the naive twenty points.
    highlight tau_lo tau_mid tau_hi

A: One number sums up how firmly the first stage stands. With one instrument, F is the squared ratio of the jump to its standard error, and ou (@highlight fs part "10" effect circle@)r county gets about ten.
    morph fs tex "F = \\left(\\frac{{60}}{{19}}\\right)^2 \\approx 10"

    erase st_axis st_t0 st_t60 st_t120 st_cap st_dot st_bar st_bar_lab tau_head tau_lo tau_mid tau_hi

A: Put F on a line that grows tenfold at each step.
    draw fscale

A: Ten is the famous threshold. Staiger and Stock offered it in 1997 as a rule of thumb: above ten, the drag toward the naive answer stays small.
    draw fscale_marker_1

B: And with one instrument, does ten also make the usual test (@erase fs@) honest, the estimate plus or minus 1.96 standard errors?
    highlight fscale_marker_1 effect circle

    ask "Where must F be for the usual 95 percent interval to be honest? Set it on the line." intro "Your guess first." on fscale store g relative true voice b
        right: Close: you said {g:0}, and the answer is a hundred and four point seven.
        wrong: You said {g:0}; the answer is a hundred and four point seven, ten times the old rule.

A: That number comes from Lee, McCrary, Moreira and Porter, in 2022. Keep the old threshold of  (@draw crit@)ten, and an honest test needs 3.43 standard errors, not 1.96.
    highlight fscale_answer effect circle

A: Applied to sixty-one papers in the American Economic Review, their correction makes the standard errors at least half again as large in a quarter of the specifications.
    highlight crit

B: Then the fix is easy. Demand  (@draw camp1 camp1n@)an F over a hundred.
    erase crit

A: Angrist and Kolesár say no. Screening studies on F biases them in its own way, because the samples that (@point at.ref fscale_marker_1 gesture tap@) pass are those whose first stage came out big by luck.
    text camp2 "Screen on sign only" #b5482e cites ["angrist2024"] x 720 y 250 font_size 40
    text camp2n "Angrist & Kolesár 2024" #8f887c x 720 y 205 font_size 30

A: With one instrument, they argue, the bias is usually small and the usual intervals cover well unless the hidden selection is extreme. They ask only that the first stage has the expected sign.
    highlight camp2

B: So which camp is right?
    highlight camp1 camp2 effect underline

A: It turns on a number no one sees: how hard frailty pushes on both treatment and death. Lee's team guard against the worst case; Angrist and Kolesár argue real studies rarely come near it.
    point at.ref fscale_answer gesture circle

A: Angrist and Kolesár add that the unseen number leaves a trace: the gap between the naive and the instrumente (@highlight camp1@)d estimate, which anyone can see, an (@highlight camp2@)d in typical studies it is moderate.
    highlight camp2 effect underline

A: So where do both camps  (@draw fscale_marker_2@)relax? Far out to the right, where a lottery like Oregon's 2008 Medicaid draw sits: by our own rough count, a first s (@highlight fscale_marker_2 effect circle@)tage of about twenty-five points over tens of thousands of people puts its F in the thousands.

## Worse than doing nothing?
voice: male
level: advanced
use: equation_plot
with:
    equation: y = 1.5*sqrt(F)*exp(-0.5*((x - 5 - 15/(F + 1))*sqrt(F)/40)^2)
    params:
      F: {value: 100, min: 2, max: 100, step: 1, label: first-stage F}
    show_equation: false
    controls: panel
    drag: F
    x_range:
      - 0
      - 45
    y_range:
      - 0
      - 16
    x_label: deaths prevented (points)
    y_label: ' '
    marks:
      - {kind: vline, at: 5, id: truth}
      - {kind: vline, at: 20, label: naive, id: naive}
      - {kind: vline, at: 5 + 15/(F + 1), id: centre}
sources:
    - id: bjb1995
      title: Problems with Instrumental Variables Estimation When the Correlation Between the Instruments and the Endogenous Explanatory Variable is Weak
      authors: Bound, Jaeger and Baker, Journal of the American Statistical Association
      year: 1995
      finding: Weak instruments magnify small flaws into large bias
    - id: mcclellan1994
      title: Does more intensive treatment of acute myocardial infarction in the elderly reduce mortality? Analysis using instrumental variables
      authors: McClellan, McNeil and Newhouse, JAMA
      year: 1994
      doi: 10.1001/jama.1994.03520110039026
      finding: 'Medicare AMI 1987–91: at most 5 points, gained on day one'
takeaways:
    - No back road from instrument to outcome
    - A weak first stage magnifies leaks
    - Ask for F and a robust interval

    scratch report work ["First stage and F","Anderson–Rubin interval","Naive beside IV"] x 820 y 300 font_size 32 hidden true
    box e_z "Distance" #4f8a4b x 170 y 430 font_size 42 hidden true
    box e_d "Cath lab" #2f6b8f x 500 y 430 font_size 42 hidden true
    box e_y "Death" x 830 y 430 font_size 42 hidden true
    arrow e_zd e_z -> e_d #4f8a4b style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    arrow e_dy e_d -> e_y style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    path e_road smooth dashed thick #b5482e points [[170,385],[260,290],[500,260],[740,290],[815,375]] hidden true
    arrow e_road_tip thick #b5482e from.x 795 from.y 345 to.x 822 to.y 388 hidden true
    text e_lab "day-one care" #b5482e cites ["mcclellan1994"] x 500 y 220 font_size 40 hidden true
    text rd_truth "truth: 5" #8f887c x 830 y 640 font_size 40 hidden true
    text rd_mid "middle: {eq.centre}" #8a5fa8 x 830 y 580 font_size 44 hidden true

    card title "Worse than doing nothing?"

A: So what does a weak instrument do to the answer? And is it really worse than not trying at all?
    draw axes x_label

    hide y_ticks y_label

A: Keep our heart-attack numbers, and this time let distance be perfectly valid, so the procedure truly prevents five deaths in a hundr (@draw naive naive_label@)ed; the naive comparison, fooled by frailty, says twenty.
    draw truth rd_truth

A: Now imagine rerunning the study again and again. With a strong first s (@draw panel@)tage, an F of a hundred, the estimates pile up tightly on the truth; the purple line marks their middle.
    draw curve_0 centre rd_mid

A: Shrink it to an F of ten, like our county: the pile spreads out, but its middle moves only a little, to about six.
    animate params.F.value 10 duration 4

A: At an F of two the middle sits at ten, a third of the way to the naive twenty, and the pile is so wide that one study could land almost anywhere.
    animate params.F.value 2 delivery grave duration 4

A: This picture is the worst case, with strong hidden selection, and the real pile also has fatter tails, with a wild estimate now and then.
    highlight curve_0

A: Try it yourself: move F with the slider, or grab the curve, and watch where the middle of the pile sits as F falls.
    explore params ["F"] watch "Watch F run between a hundred and two, and the pile walk toward the naive answer."

A: As F falls the pile spreads first; only well below ten does its middle slide far toward the naive twenty.
    animate params.F.value 10 duration 3

B: But at an F of two the interval would be enormous. Who would be fooled?
    highlight curve_0 effect pulse

A: Not always. The usual standard error is built from the same shaky first stage, and when the hidden selection is strong it can come out too small, so a tight interval sits around a wrong number.
    highlight curve_0

A: That's the sense in which weak can be worse than nothing. The naive twenty points you would distrust on sight; a weak instrument hands back something close to it, labelled causal.
    highlight naive naive_label

B: And if the instrument is a little invalid as well?
    highlight truth rd_truth

A: Then any leak, like that first-day care, is divided by a tiny first stage. Bound, Jaeger and Baker made the (@highlight naive naive_label@) point in 1995: a weak instrument turns a small flaw into a large bias.
    animate params.F.value 3 duration 3

A: The defence is to show the first stage and its F, an interv (@draw report_line_2@)al that stays honest when F is small, such as Anderson and Rubin's, (@draw report_line_3@) and the naive estimate beside the IV one.
    draw report_box report_line_1

    erase report

    quiz "A study instruments same-day heart surgery with weekend admission. The first-stage F is 12, and the estimate says surgery halves deaths. What should worry you most?" intro "One last case." voice b
        right: Weekend staffing: fewer staff at weekends is a road to survival that skips the surgery, and a first stage with an F of twelve magnifies any such leak.
        wrong: Twelve is above ten, but ask what else differs at a weekend besides the surgery.
        * Nothing: twelve passes the rule
        + Weekend staffing
        * The F should be above a hundred

    clear

A: So can an accident of address tell us whether catheterization saves lives? Only if it reaches death through nothing but the lab.
    draw takeaway_1

A: And only if it moves enough patients, because a weak first stage magnifies every leak and slides toward the naive answer.
    draw takeaway_2

A: So ask any instrument for its first stage, its F, and an interval that survives a weak one.
    draw takeaway_3

    erase takeaway_1 takeaway_2 takeaway_3

A: And the 1994 study shows why. Its own day-one finding hinted at a (@draw e_road e_road_tip e_lab@) back road from address to survival, s (@highlight e_road e_lab@)o the honest reading was five points at most, perhaps none of it from the procedure.
    draw e_z e_zd e_d e_dy e_y

## Where next
end_page: true

    text end_next_kicker "Next" x 500 y 540 font_size 26 style.opacity 0.6
    link end_next href lecture:9 title "Selection on observables: matching, regression, and the leap of faith" form title size 600 x 500 y 420

