audio:
  lang: en
  lines:
    male|a||Live a few miles closer to a hospital with a cardiac catheterization lab, and after a heart attack you are more likely to get the procedure. Can that accident of address tell us whether the procedure saves lives?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 13320
      voice: gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed
    male|b||Why bother with geography? Why not just compare the patients who got the procedure with those who didn't?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 5440
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow #ctx:l7pxr8'
    male|a||Here is what that comparison looks like, in numbers made up to be easy. Of a thousand catheterized patients, a hundred and fifty die within a year; of a thousand who weren't, three hundred and fifty.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 12640
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:1j3m0j6'
    male|b||Two hundred fewer deaths per thousand, twenty points. On those numbers the procedure is a miracle.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 6920
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow #ctx:1eyxe2p'
    male|a||Mostly it's a selection. Cardiologists send to the lab the patients strong enough to go, and those patients would have lived longer anyway.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 8440
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:22i4kt'
    male|a||So McClellan, McNeil and Newhouse sorted Medicare heart-attack patients aged sixty-five and over, from 1987 to 1991, by distance instead, and followed their survival for up to four years.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 12880
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:886j40'
    male|a||Near means the nearest catheterization hospital is hardly farther than the nearest hospital of any kind. Far means it is a real detour.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 9760
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:ymshp0'
    male|b||And distance really changes who gets the procedure?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 3040
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow #ctx:11dmkj8'
    male|a||It does. Keeping to round numbers, two hundred and sixty of every thousand near patients are catheterized, against two hundred of the far ones.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 10960
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:nmkln4'
    'male|a||That''s sixty extra procedures per thousand, caused by nothing but the address: the first stage. We also assume living closer never makes anyone less likely to get it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11560
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:o3ltvz'
    male|b||If the procedure works, the near group should die less. Do they?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 4400
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow #ctx:1nscx4v'
    male|a||A little. Three hundred deaths against three hundred and three.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 4520
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:pv6rlr'
    male|a||Three fewer deaths per thousand, which is called the reduced form.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 4520
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:4qbh12'
    male|a||Now divide. Three fewer deaths, spread over sixty extra procedures, is one life for every twenty patients the distance moved.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 9800
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:1gwjbrq'
    male|a||Five percentage points, for the patients whose treatment hung on their address. Our counts are made up; the real study found a survival gain of at most five points, up to four years on.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 11280
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:vire6x'
    'male|a||That ratio is the instrumental-variables estimate: the jump in deaths between the groups, over the jump in treatment.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7000
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:w7163y'
    male|b||Five points, not twenty. But you're assuming those three deaths came only through the sixty procedures.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 6800
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow #ctx:1tym8wz'
    male|a||Yes, and everything hangs on that word, only. A hospital with a lab is more than a lab.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 6400
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:gdgee7'
    male|a||Draw the study as arrows. Distance pushes some patients into the lab, and the lab may change who dies.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 8360
      voice: gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed
    'male|a||Frailty, which no claims form records, pushes on both: it keeps people out of the lab, and it kills them. That''s the poison in the naive twenty points.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11080
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:1ac2md2'
    male|b||And distance gets round frailty how?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 3240
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow #ctx:1pexdjz'
    male|a||Only if two things hold. First, distance must have nothing to do with frailty, as if each address had been drawn by lottery.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 8200
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:1eul70p'
    male|a||The authors' case was that few people choose a home by its distance to a cath lab, and that near and far patients looked alike on the health measures in their records.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 9360
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:1noszqs'
    male|a||Second, distance may reach death only through the lab, with no other road from the address to survival. That's the exclusion restriction.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 9240
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:e9gjdb'
    male|b||That one sounds harder. A hospital with a lab is usually a big, busy hospital.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 5640
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow #ctx:18crpih'
    male|a||It is, and it may do everything faster on the first day, not just catheterize. Far patients also tend to be rural, a long way from every kind of care.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 11520
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:ezx0sd'
    male|a||And the study found a trace of exactly this. The survival gain showed up on the first day in hospital, too early to be the procedures, and the authors put it down to other treatments.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 10720
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:1shbp4i'
    male|b||So how much damage can a small leak like that do?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 3080
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow #ctx:1uscihu'
    male|a||Back to our thousand patients. Suppose all three fewer deaths came from first-day care, and the lab itself saves no one.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 7840
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:3qq777'
    male|a||The ratio can't tell the difference. It still divides three by sixty and reports five points, for a procedure that does nothing.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 8720
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:bkaoq1'
    male|a||In general, the estimate is the true effect plus the leak, delta, divided by the first stage, pi.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 7640
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:1os5ane'
    male|b||So the smaller the first stage, the bigger the same leak becomes.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 4920
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow #ctx:1o3bwym'
    male|a||Divide the same three deaths by six extra procedures instead of sixty, and the leak turns into fifty points.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 8120
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:19q0u9h'
    male|b||A new case. A study uses each doctor's choice for the previous patient, drug A or B, as an instrument for the drug the next patient gets. Which story would break the exclusion restriction?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 14320
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow #ctx:1eo6rfm'
    'male|b||The follow-up: it is a second road from the doctor''s habit to the patient''s health that skips the drug, so the ratio would book it as the drug''s effect.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10080
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow'
    male|a||That's why exclusion is an argument, never a test result. The data show the first stage, but the road from address to death never shows up in them.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 10400
      voice: gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed
    'male|a||The ratio has a second weak spot, underneath it: the sixty extra procedures are themselves an estimate, with noise.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8160
      voice: gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed
    male|a||With all of Medicare behind it, the 1994 study had a strong first stage. But run the same design in a single county, two thousand patients, and sixty per thousand comes with a standard error of about nineteen.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 13280
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:6bki9z'
    male|b||Nineteen either way of sixty. So in another sample, the jump could come out anywhere from about twenty to a hundred.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 8840
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow #ctx:1jykld0'
    male|a||Now look at the ratio underneath. Three deaths over sixty procedures is five points; over a hundred, about three; but over twenty-two, fourteen. The ratio swings hardest on the low side.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 14600
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:1z0lcab'
    male|a||Worse, the swings lean one way. In a small sample the same hidden frailty jiggles the first stage and the deaths together, so the noise drags the estimate toward the naive twenty points.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 12920
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:i1396l'
    male|a||One number sums up how firmly the first stage stands. With one instrument, F is the squared ratio of the jump to its standard error, and our county gets about ten.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 11400
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:o0g0tn'
    male|a||Put F on a line that grows tenfold at each step.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 4160
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:4k1sd3'
    'male|a||Ten is the famous threshold. Staiger and Stock offered it in 1997 as a rule of thumb: above ten, the drag toward the naive answer stays small.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10720
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:yg86jw'
    male|b||And with one instrument, does ten also make the usual test honest, the estimate plus or minus 1.96 standard errors?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 8320
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow #ctx:1bqjsrk'
    male|b||Your guess first. Where must F be for the usual 95 percent interval to be honest? Set it on the line.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 6520
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow #ctx:1lvegum'
    male|a||That number comes from Lee, McCrary, Moreira and Porter, in 2022. Keep the old threshold of ten, and an honest test needs 3.43 standard errors, not 1.96.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 13240
      voice: gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed
    male|a||Applied to sixty-one papers in the American Economic Review, their correction makes the standard errors at least half again as large in a quarter of the specifications.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 9800
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:1wej5dg'
    male|b||Then the fix is easy. Demand an F over a hundred.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 3320
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow #ctx:gwubuu'
    male|a||Angrist and Kolesár say no. Screening studies on F biases them in its own way, because the samples that pass are those whose first stage came out big by luck.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 10400
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:1j0fdlm'
    male|a||With one instrument, they argue, the bias is usually small and the usual intervals cover well unless the hidden selection is extreme. They ask only that the first stage has the expected sign.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 12880
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:10zlz8q'
    male|b||So which camp is right?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 2160
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow #ctx:q4u3to'
    'male|a||It turns on a number no one sees: how hard frailty pushes on both treatment and death. Lee''s team guard against the worst case; Angrist and Kolesár argue real studies rarely come near it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 14080
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:1l3oxot'
    'male|a||Angrist and Kolesár add that the unseen number leaves a trace: the gap between the naive and the instrumented estimate, which anyone can see, and in typical studies it is moderate.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12360
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:1gjt22z'
    'male|a||So where do both camps relax? Far out to the right, where a lottery like Oregon''s 2008 Medicaid draw sits: by our own rough count, a first stage of about twenty-five points over tens of thousands of people puts its F in the thousands.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 15560
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:n2pe7s'
    male|a||So what does a weak instrument do to the answer? And is it really worse than not trying at all?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 6640
      voice: gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed
    male|a||Keep our heart-attack numbers, and this time let distance be perfectly valid, so the procedure truly prevents five deaths in a hundred; the naive comparison, fooled by frailty, says twenty.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 12760
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:14zaoyo'
    male|a||Now imagine rerunning the study again and again. With a strong first stage, an F of a hundred, the estimates pile up tightly on the truth; the purple line marks their middle.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 12680
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:73lyn9'
    'male|a||Shrink it to an F of ten, like our county: the pile spreads out, but its middle moves only a little, to about six.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8080
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:1szgmui'
    male|a|grave|At an F of two the middle sits at ten, a third of the way to the naive twenty, and the pile is so wide that one study could land almost anywhere.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 11000
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:o4mb2t'
    male|a||This picture is the worst case, with strong hidden selection, and the real pile also has fatter tails, with a wild estimate now and then.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 9240
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:i9m5cj'
    male|a||Watch F run between a hundred and two, and the pile walk toward the naive answer.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 7040
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:tvg8xs'
    male|a||As F falls the pile spreads first; only well below ten does its middle slide far toward the naive twenty.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 8640
      voice: gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed
    male|b||But at an F of two the interval would be enormous. Who would be fooled?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 5760
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow #ctx:1y9lvvm'
    male|a||Not always. The usual standard error is built from the same shaky first stage, and when the hidden selection is strong it can come out too small, so a tight interval sits around a wrong number.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 14800
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:1bipzns'
    male|a||That's the sense in which weak can be worse than nothing. The naive twenty points you would distrust on sight; a weak instrument hands back something close to it, labelled causal.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 11680
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:1tkxhbj'
    male|b||And if the instrument is a little invalid as well?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 5080
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow #ctx:1ag606j'
    'male|a||Then any leak, like that first-day care, is divided by a tiny first stage. Bound, Jaeger and Baker made the point in 1995: a weak instrument turns a small flaw into a large bias.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 15240
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:1dpw3fx'
    male|a||The defence is to show the first stage and its F, an interval that stays honest when F is small, such as Anderson and Rubin's, and the naive estimate beside the IV one.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 11280
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:1qhh1tj'
    male|b||One last case. A study instruments same-day heart surgery with weekend admission. The first-stage F is 12, and the estimate says surgery halves deaths. What should worry you most?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 14240
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow #ctx:ldwb71'
    'male|b||Weekend staffing: fewer staff at weekends is a road to survival that skips the surgery, and a first stage with an F of twelve magnifies any such leak.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11280
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow'
    male|a||So can an accident of address tell us whether catheterization saves lives? Only if it reaches death through nothing but the lab.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 10040
      voice: gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed
    male|a||And only if it moves enough patients, because a weak first stage magnifies every leak and slides toward the naive answer.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 8360
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:13gljc7'
    male|a||So ask any instrument for its first stage, its F, and an interval that survives a weak one.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 6600
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:1yi3bk7'
    male|a||And the 1994 study shows why. Its own day-one finding hinted at a back road from address to survival, so the honest reading was five points at most, perhaps none of it from the procedure.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 12880
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed #ctx:i80fgt'
    male|b||Two of these are reasons the instrument works. Which one opens a road to health that skips the drug?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 7000
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow'
    male|a||Yes.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 1160
      voice: gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed
    male|b||Yes.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 2360
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow'
    male|a||Right.:
      mp3: 
